    "yamlSchema": "name: erp-dashboard-design\nversion: \"1.0.0\"\ndescription: \"Design dense, uncluttered back-office, ERP, dashboard, and internal-tool UI/UX.\"\nparameters:\n  type: object\n  properties:\n    moduleName:\n      type: string\n      description: \"ERP module or admin panel screen\"\nrequired:\n  - moduleName",
    "skillMd": "---\nname: erp-dashboard-design\ndescription: Use this skill whenever the user is designing, redesigning, or reviewing ERP, back-office, admin, or internal-tool UI/UX — dashboards, data tables, forms, module navigation, or any \"boring but important\" enterprise software screen. Trigger this for requests like \"design an ERP dashboard\", \"make this admin panel cleaner\", \"how should I lay out this inventory/HR/finance module\", or any dense-data interface that needs to feel neat, uncluttered, and usable by non-technical staff for hours a day. Covers information architecture, layout grids, data table density, form patterns, color/status tokens, and concrete spacing/typography values — not general marketing-site or landing-page design (for that, defer to frontend-design).\n---\n\n# ERP / Enterprise Software UX-UI Design\n\nERP software lives or dies on one thing: can a tired employee find and finish their task at 4pm on a Friday without thinking. This skill is a set of defaults, not decoration — every section below exists to remove a decision that otherwise gets made inconsistently across screens.\n\nUse this skill for anything internal/operational: inventory, HR, finance, CRM-as-ERP-module, warehouse, procurement, project ops, admin panels. Always pair it with `frontend-design` for the underlying visual craft (type pairing, color theory) — this skill governs the *structural* decisions specific to dense, data-heavy software.\n\n## 1. Design philosophy — the four rules\n\nState these up front to the user if they haven't given constraints; they anchor every later decision.\n\n1. **Density over decoration.** ERP users are power users doing repetitive tasks. Optimize for scan speed and click-count, not visual flourish. Whitespace is used to group, not to impress.\n2. **Predictability beats novelty.** Every module should share the same shell, the same table pattern, the same form pattern. A user who learns one screen should already know 80% of every other screen.\n3. **Status is always visible.** ERP data has state (draft/pending/approved/overdue/error). Status must be scannable via color + icon + label — never color alone.\n4. **Never lose the user's place.** Filters, sort order, and scroll position persist across navigation. Destructive actions always confirm. Long-running actions always show progress.\n\n## 2. Information architecture\n\n- **Shell = persistent sidebar + top bar + content area.** Don't reinvent per module.\n  - Sidebar: 240–280px expanded, collapsible to 64px icon rail. Group by function (e.g. Sales, Inventory, Finance, HR), not by database table.\n  - Top bar (56–64px): global search, notifications, org/workspace switcher, user menu. Never put primary actions here — those live in the content area.\n  - Content area: max content width 1440–1600px on ultra-wide monitors; don't let tables stretch edge-to-edge on 27\"+ displays, cap and center, or let side panels absorb the extra width.\n- **Navigation depth: max 3 levels.** Module → sub-module → record. If you need a 4th level, it's a filter or tab on level 3, not a new nav item.\n- **Breadcrumbs are mandatory below 2 levels of depth.** Format: `Module / Sub-module / Record name`.\n- **Every module needs a landing/list view, not a dashboard-first pattern.** Users entering \"Purchase Orders\" want the PO list, not a chart. Save dashboards for the home screen and dedicated analytics views."
